      "source": [
        "## **PART 1 - What Is an LLM**\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context\n",
        "\n",
        "Before we look at architecture, we need the idea.\n",
        "\n",
        "A language model is a probability distribution over sequences of tokens. That is it. Given a sequence of tokens so far, it assigns a probability to every possible next token. The highest-probability token is the model's prediction for the next word. This is the entire operating principle of an LLM.\n",
        "\n",
        "An LLM - a large language model - is what happens when you scale that probability machine to billions of parameters and train it on a significant fraction of human text. At sufficient scale, the model starts to capture general structure: grammar, semantics, reasoning patterns, code, and even abstract problem-solving.\n",
        "\n",
        "The word \"large\" is doing real work here. GPT-2 (2019, 1.5B params) could write coherent paragraphs. GPT-3 (2020, 175B params) could do few-shot in-context learning. GPT-4 (2023, estimated >1T params in internal mixtures) could perform much more complex reasoning and multi-step task execution.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### What an LLM Outputs\n",
        "\n",
        "**Simple**\n",
        "An LLM does not \"think\" or \"know\" things the way a human does. At every step, it looks at all the text so far and asks: what word comes next? It runs that calculation billions of times per response. The output is not a fact lookup but a probability estimate over the next token.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "An LLM is a parameterised function $f_\\theta$ where $\\theta$ represents hundreds of billions of floating-point weights. It is trained via next-token prediction: given tokens $t_1, t_2, ..., t_n$, the model learns $P(t_{n+1} | t_1, ..., t_n)$. This is essentially a conditional probability model over text.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why Scale Works\n",
        "\n",
        "**Simple**\n",
        "Imagine you trained on every book, website, research paper, and forum post ever written in English. You would pick up not just vocabulary but how arguments are structured, how code patterns repeat, how narratives flow, and how knowledge is connected. Larger models acquire this structure more reliably.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "The scaling laws literature (Kaplan et al. 2020, Hoffmann et al. 2022 \"Chinchilla\") showed that loss decreases predictably as a power law with model size (N), dataset size (D), and compute budget (C). In other words, increasing model size and data usually yields steadily improving performance on language tasks."
      ],
      "id": "cpINEhzfR8b4"
    },
