      "source": [
        "## **PART 2 - NLP History: The Road to Transformers**\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context\n",
        "\n",
        "Transformers did not appear from nowhere. To understand why self-attention was a breakthrough, you need to understand what it replaced and why that approach broke. The history runs: bag-of-words -> embeddings -> RNN/LSTM -> attention -> Transformer.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Word2Vec (2013)\n",
        "\n",
        "**Word2vec** (Mikolov et al. 2013) learned to represent words as points in a high-dimensional space so that similar words ended up close together - the classic demo is king - man + woman = queen. This gave us dense vector representations of words, which was a major leap over one-hot vectors.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### RNNs and LSTMs (2014-2016)\n",
        "\n",
        "**RNNs** process a sentence one word at a time, left to right, carrying a hidden state that updates with each word. The problem: that hidden state is a fixed-size vector, and information from earlier tokens has to be compressed into it. LSTMs improved this with gates, but the fundamental limitation remained.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### The Sequential Bottleneck\n",
        "\n",
        "GPUs are fast because they do thousands of operations simultaneously. RNNs/LSTMs are forced to process one token after another, each step waiting for the previous one - you are throwing away the parallelism that makes GPUs useful.\n",
        "\n",
        "**The key insight that led to Transformers:** word2vec gave fixed vectors, RNNs had a sequential bottleneck, and the fixed-size context vector was an information bottleneck. Transformers solved both by letting every token interact directly with every other token in parallel."
      ],
      "id": "ZhIbXqZIR8b5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
