        "## **PART 3 - Attention Is All You Need (Vaswani et al. 2017)**\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context\n",
        "\n",
        "In 2015, Bahdanau et al. introduced attention as an add-on to seq2seq RNNs. Instead of compressing everything into one vector, the decoder could \"look back\" at encoder hidden states with different weights depending on which part of the input mattered most.\n",
        "\n",
        "But attention was still bolted onto RNNs. You still had the sequential bottleneck. The computation on each token still had to wait for the previous one.\n",
        "\n",
        "The insight in \"Attention Is All You Need\" (Vaswani et al. 2017) was: what if you threw away the RNN entirely and used only attention? Attention can be computed for all pairs of positions in parallel, so the model can process a full sentence at once.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### The Insight\n",
        "\n",
        "**Simple**\n",
        "Instead of reading a sentence left to right and updating a running memory, a Transformer looks at all words simultaneously and lets each word decide which other words it needs to pay attention to.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "The self-attention operation computes relationships between all positions in a sequence simultaneously. For a sequence of length $n$ with $d_{model}$ dimensions, this is a matrix of $n \\times n$ attention scores, and those scores are computed in parallel.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Self-Attention Mechanics\n",
        "\n",
        "**Simple**\n",
        "For each word, self-attention asks three questions: \"What am I looking for?\" (Query), \"What do I offer to others?\" (Key), \"What is my actual content?\" (Value). To compute attention for a word, we compare its Query to every other word's Key, then weight the other words' Values by those scores.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "Given input matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times d_{model}}$, three learnable projection matrices $W^Q, W^K, W^V$ map $X$ to query, key, and value matrices:\n",
        "$$Q = XW^Q, \\quad K = XW^K, \\quad V = XW^V$$\n",
        "Attention output is:\n",
        "$$\\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V$$\n",
        "The division by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ prevents the dot products from growing large in magnitude as dimension increases (which would push softmax into near-zero gradient regions). Multi-head attention runs this operation multiple times in parallel, each with a different learned projection.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Encoder-Only vs Decoder-Only\n",
        "\n",
        "**Simple**\n",
        "The original Transformer had two parts: an encoder that reads the full input and builds a rich understanding of it, and a decoder that generates the output one token at a time while looking at the encoder output and previous generated tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "Encoder-only models (like BERT) are used when you need to understand/classify existing text. Decoder-only models (GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama) are used when you need to generate text.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "Encoder-only models (BERT, RoBERTa) use bidirectional attention: every token can attend to every other token in both directions. They are trained with masked language modelling (MLM) and next-sentence objectives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Decoder-only models (GPT family, Claude, Llama, Mistral) use causal (masked) self-attention: each token can only attend to tokens at earlier positions. This constraint is required for autoregressive generation, where the model predicts the next token from the prefix."
      ],
      "id": "CkDpsDpBR8b7"
