      },
      "source": [
        "## **PART 5 - Inference Optimisations**\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context\n",
        "\n",
        "Running LLMs at scale requires significant engineering beyond the base Transformer. Two techniques are particularly important: Flash Attention (which makes attention computation faster and more memory efficient) and continuous batching (which improves throughput under real workloads).\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Flash Attention\n",
        "\n",
        "Standard attention computes a full $n \\times n$ matrix of attention scores in GPU memory before applying softmax. For a 100K context, that is 10 billion numbers - far more than fits comfortably in fast memory. Flash Attention reorders the computation to avoid materializing the full matrix in high-latency memory.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why developers care:** faster inference, longer context windows possible without running out of GPU memory.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Continuous Batching\n",
        "\n",
        "Early serving systems processed one batch at a time - start all together, wait for all to finish. If some requests finish early, those GPU slots sit idle. Continuous batching (also called iteration-level scheduling) keeps the GPU busy by constantly admitting new requests as others finish.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why developers care:** 2-10x throughput improvement over static batching in production workloads - directly reduces inference cost per request."
      ],
      "id": "NqSApK7pR8b9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "oCZPpBiQR8b-"
      },
      "source": [
        "## **PART 6 - Mixture of Experts (MoE)**\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context\n",
        "\n",
        "As models scaled toward 1T+ parameters, a problem emerged: every forward pass activates all parameters for every token, regardless of whether those parameters are relevant. Mixture of Experts (MoE) addresses this by routing each token to only a small subset of expert networks.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why it matters:** you get a large model's knowledge capacity at a smaller model's inference cost - this is how trillion-parameter-scale models are practical to run."
      ],
      "id": "oCZPpBiQR8b-"
    },
    {
