      "source": [
        "## **PART 7 - Reasoning Models**\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context\n",
        "\n",
        "Standard LLMs generate the answer token by token in a single forward pass (with possible chain-of-thought). The compute budget is roughly fixed by the prompt length and output length. Reasoning models change that by spending more test-time compute before returning the final answer.\n",
        "\n",
        "OpenAI's o1 (September 2024), o3, and Anthropic's Claude with Extended Thinking are the primary examples.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### The Core Idea\n",
        "\n",
        "**Simple**\n",
        "A standard LLM writes the answer the same way whether the question is \"What is 2+2\" or \"Prove Fermat's Last Theorem.\" A reasoning model is trained to spend time thinking - generating internal reasoning tokens before producing the final answer.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "Reasoning models are trained with reinforcement learning (specifically variants of RLHF/RLAIF/GRPO) to generate extended chain-of-thought before producing a final answer. The key properties: more inference-time compute, higher latency, and better performance on hard tasks.\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Trade-offs\n",
        "\n",
        "**Simple**\n",
        "Reasoning models are slower and more expensive per query than standard models. They are not always better - on simple questions, the extra thinking is wasted. They excel on problems with a clear objective, multiple steps, or a need for structured reasoning.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Technical**\n",
        "The latency profile of reasoning models is fundamentally different from standard models. A 30-second thinking window might generate 2000-5000 reasoning tokens at ~50 tokens/second, adding 40-100 seconds of latency. This is valuable on difficult tasks but wasteful on simple prompts."
      ],
      "id": "paJEc286R8cA"
    },
