        "# **Key Takeaways**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. An LLM is a probability distribution over next tokens. Everything else - reasoning, translation, code - is what emerges when you train that probability machine at sufficient scale on enough data.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. RNNs/LSTMs broke on two things: the sequential bottleneck (no GPU parallelism across time) and the fixed-size context vector (information loss on long sequences). Transformers solved both by allowing all tokens to interact in parallel via attention.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Self-attention computes $\\text{softmax}(QK^T / \\sqrt{d_k})V$ for all positions simultaneously. The $O(n^2)$ cost in sequence length is the fundamental constraint that drives Flash Attention and long-context engineering.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. KV cache is the core inference optimisation. Prefill (processing the prompt) is $O(n^2)$ and happens once. Decode (generating each token) is $O(n)$ per step with the cache. Without it, generation would be far slower.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. MoE (Mixture of Experts) makes large models economical. Total parameters determine knowledge capacity; active parameters per token determine compute cost. GPT-4 class models achieve trillion-scale capacity without paying the full parameter cost per token.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. Reasoning models (o1/o3/Claude Extended Thinking) introduce a third compute axis: test-time compute. Standard models have fixed inference cost. Reasoning models spend variable compute thinking before responding.\n",
        "\n",
        "7. The 2017 Transformer paper did not just introduce a new architecture - it enabled modern LLMs by removing the sequential bottleneck that made large-scale language model training impractical."
      ],
      "id": "KQfsWQTbR8cK"
